; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Even and Odd Talk to Each Other
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     mutual recursion, predicates, call stack, sequences
; SUMMARY:  Two procedures that each call the other decide whether a number is even, and then produce Hofstadter's Female and Male sequences.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Is 7 even? Only if 6 is odd. Is 6 odd? Only if 5 is even. And so
;   on down to 0, which is even. ISEVEN asks ISODD, and ISODD asks
;   ISEVEN, passing the question back and forth with a number one
;   smaller each time. This is mutual recursion: neither procedure
;   calls itself directly, but together they recurse.
;   The second part uses the same trick for two number sequences
;   invented by Douglas Hofstadter, where each one is defined using
;   the other.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and read the conversation that is printed.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the  output isodd :n - 1  line in ISEVEN and
;      run. Open the Call Stack and press F5 a few times. The stack is a
;      ladder that alternates ISEVEN, ISODD, ISEVEN, ISODD.
;   3. Click down the stack and watch :n count up as you go older.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * mutual recursion between two procedures
;   * predicates that output true or false
;   * every recursion needs a way to reach a stopping case
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Ask iseven 2000. How tall does the stack get?
;   * What happens with iseven -1? Add a rule that handles negative
;     numbers by making them positive first.
;   * Make three procedures that pass a number around in a circle to
;     find the remainder after dividing by 3.
; ============================================================

; 0 is even. Any other number is even when the number before it is odd.
to iseven :n
  if :n = 0 [output "true]
  output isodd :n - 1
end

; 0 is not odd. Any other number is odd when the number before it is even.
to isodd :n
  if :n = 0 [output "false]
  output iseven :n - 1
end

; The same pair, but they print the conversation. :pad is the indent.
to talk.even :n :pad
  if :n = 0 [print (word :pad "|0 is even. Yes!|) output "true]
  print (word :pad "|Is | :n "| even? Ask whether | :n - 1 "| is odd.|)
  output talk.odd :n - 1 word :pad "| |
end

to talk.odd :n :pad
  if :n = 0 [print (word :pad "|0 is not odd. No!|) output "false]
  print (word :pad "|Is | :n "| odd? Ask whether | :n - 1 "| is even.|)
  output talk.even :n - 1 word :pad "| |
end

; Hofstadter's sequences: F(0) = 1, M(0) = 0, and then
;   F(n) = n - M(F(n - 1))      M(n) = n - F(M(n - 1))
to female :n
  if :n = 0 [output 1]
  output :n - male female :n - 1
end

to male :n
  if :n = 0 [output 0]
  output :n - female male :n - 1
end

print [A conversation between ISEVEN and ISODD:]
print sentence [So is 5 even?] talk.even 5 "
print []
foreach [0 1 8 13 100] [print (sentence ? [is even:] iseven ? [and odd:] isodd ?)]
print []
print [Hofstadter's Female and Male sequences:]
print sentence "F: map [female ?] iseq 0 15
print sentence "M: map [male ?] iseq 0 15
